[
"# F1 Scope v2 — everything, at zero cost\n\n2026-09-28 · @Mohammad AlFalasi\n\nF1 can hold every civilisation, culture, polity, movement, technique, material, object type and living practice the field names, and pay nothing for it. The canon register now lists 2,430 of them, with 198 free source routes and 130 primary texts. \"Everything\" means every one is on the Atlas and findable; how deeply each is taught follows the evidence, not fame.\n\n## What the last recommendation got wrong\n\nFour points in my last notes (tasks 25–27) misread the project's own documents. They are withdrawn here; everything else in them is carried into §10.\n\n| Last recommendation | What was wrong | Replaced by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Add 3–5 civilisation briefs (Nok, Chola, Iznik, Jingdezhen, Benin) | Too few, and it ignored Atlas Logic Q7: \"civilisation\" is a labelled grouping and a search name, never the frame | Every one of 93 civilisation names gets a caveat card and a grouping page (§3) |\n",
"| F1.5–F1.13 are thematic chapters (\"F1.6 metals, F1.7 fibre arts…\") | F1.6–F1.12a are world chapters, each read through one device (Africa through metallurgy, the Americas through fibre); only F1.5 and F1.13 are cross-world | Each world chapter keeps its device; breadth goes into world hubs (§5) |\n| \"Living practices: 1 object\" | The practices register already held 12 (PR-001 to PR-018) | 214 living practices in the canon; each world chapter ends on one |\n| Show funerary objects if framed as practice | Stated too loosely: \"framed as practice\" is not a test | The contextual rule already before you as Groundwork Q21: never remains, sacred or secret material, or what a descendant community asks not to show; other grave goods shown with a content note. The canon's 287 funerary flags feed that check |\n| Budget for Hermitage permissions and paid sensitivity reads (Deep Dives 2 Q16; Phase 4 item 15, option A) | Breaks the zero-cost rule (decision 8) | Free routes for each (§7) |\n\n## What \"everything\" means\n\n",
"Everything is reachable because the Atlas already has three depths. A node costs one register row at R3, a card and member grid at R2, and an authored walk only at R1. So the canon can list every entity the field names, and effort goes where a unit teaches.\n\n- **R1:** taught in a unit, with a grouping page and a 6–9 step walk.\n- **R2:** a card and a member grid; needs five or more verified members and a source.\n- **R3:** a card generated from the register row, linking out to Wikidata, PeriodO, Getty or the UNESCO graph.\n\nEvery node starts at R3 at least. It moves up when its evidence is verified, never because it is famous.\n\n| Family | Nodes | R1 | R2 | R3 | How it reaches all of it |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Civilisation names | 93 | 0 → 13 | 12 → 80 | 81 → 0 | Every name is raised to R2: its members are the canon groupings beneath it, which already exist (§3) |\n| Archaeological cultures and horizons | 247 | 4 | 56 | 187 | From the Lower Palaeolithic to the Thule; PeriodO holds competing definitions |\n| Polities and dynasties | 337 | 63 | 191 | 83 | Cliopatria's borders layer maps the rest; stateless peoples stay visible through cultures and communities |\n",
"| Belief traditions | 55 | 11 | 29 | 15 | As lenses on every surface |\n| Movements, styles and schools | 333 | 21 | 92 | 220 | All regions; 106 declared movements, 180 styles, 47 schools |\n| Maker communities, institutions and exhibitions | 218 | 8 | 69 | 141 | Guilds, karkhanas, manufactories, academies, the waqf, world's fairs, biennales |\n| Networks, interaction spheres and diasporas | 78 | 9 | 21 | 48 | Goods, materials, skills and people as separate layers; people never drawn as goods |\n| Techniques | 230 | 13 | 48 | 169 | Each as an operational sequence (the autopsy's fifth layer) |\n| Materials | 128 | 6 | 6 | 116 | With source regions and extraction and labour notes |\n| Object types | 323 | 31 | 144 | 148 | Every one of the 12 functions has at least 29 types |\n| Living practices | 214 | 17 | 94 | 103 | UNESCO status is taken from the ICH dataset, never typed |\n| \"First known\" claims | 174 | 22 | 70 | 82 | One row per independent invention, each with a confidence |\n| **All** | **2,430** | **205** | **832** | **1,393** |  |\n\nThe civilisation row shows the change I recommend (Q29). The other rows are the tiers as drafted.\n\n",
"The canon is the minimum, not the ceiling. Beyond it, Ring 3 search reaches every open record in the connected collections, and anyone can propose a new row. A row is added with a source, never with a guessed identifier.\n\n## Civilisations: all 93 names\n\nEvery name a learner might search for as \"X civilisation\" gets a card and a grouping page. None is left as a bare search result. This keeps Atlas Logic Q7 intact: the word stays a label that opens onto the precise groupings beneath it, never the Atlas's frame. Covering all of them is cheap because the members already exist in the canon: the polities, cultures, horizons and networks each name is built from.\n\nEach card carries four things: who uses the name, what sits underneath, what the name leaves out, and the argument about it. Nok's card, for example, says the name implies a state where the evidence shows farming communities. The \"Western\" card lists the African, Islamic and Byzantine roots the label borrows from and the colonised makers whose objects fill its museums.\n\n| World | Count | Names |\n|---|---|---|\n",
"| Africa (F1.6) | 17 | Egyptian; Nubian / Kushite; Aksumite; Ethiopian (Abyssinian); Swahili; Great Zimbabwe; Mapungubwe; Nok; Ife; Benin; Kongo; Ghana / Wagadu; Mali; Songhay; Kanem-Bornu; Carthaginian / Punic; Garamantian |\n| The Americas (F1.7) | 26 | Olmec; Maya; Teotihuacan; Zapotec; Mixtec; Toltec; Aztec / Mexica; Purépecha / Tarascan; Mesoamerican; Caral / Norte Chico; Chavín; Moche; Nazca; Tiwanaku; Wari; Chimú; Inca; Andean; Muisca; Taíno; Marajoara; Amazonian \"garden cities\"; Ancestral Puebloan; Mississippian; Mound Builders; Pre-Columbian |\n| East Asia (F1.8) | 3 | Chinese; Japanese; Korean |\n| South and Southeast Asia (F1.9) | 8 | Indus Valley; Vedic; Gandharan; Tibetan; Khmer; Cham (Champa); Pyu; Indianised states of Southeast Asia |\n| West Asia before Islam (F1.9a) | 15 | Mesopotamian; Sumerian; Babylonian; Assyrian; Elamite; Hittite; Urartian; Phoenician; Persian; Dilmun; Magan; Sabaean; Nabataean; Jiroft; Helmand |\n| The Islamic world (F1.10) | 1 | Islamic |\n",
"| Europe and the Mediterranean (F1.11) | 15 | Cycladic; Minoan; Mycenaean; Ancient Greek; Hellenistic; Etruscan; Roman; Greek and Roman; Byzantine; Celtic; Thracian; Nuragic; Norse / Viking; Old Europe; Moorish |\n| Australia and the Pacific (F1.12) | 4 | Lapita; Polynesian; Rapa Nui; Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander |\n| The steppe and Central Asia (F1.12a) | 3 | Scythian; Sogdian; Oxus |\n| Thematic (F1.3) | 1 | Western |\n\nUmbrella and loaded labels (Mound Builders, Pre-Columbian, Old Europe, Moorish, Western) are kept on purpose. Learners search for them, and their cards teach why the label is a problem. \"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander\" is not a self-description as a civilisation. Its card points first to the more than 250 language groups' own names and carries the consultation notice.\n\nThirteen names get a walk (R1), because a unit teaches through them:\n\n- The eight already seeded: Indus Valley, Egyptian, Maya, Andean, Chinese, Islamic, Greek and Roman, Swahili.\n- Western, for F1.3's \"civilisation as an argument\".\n- Great Zimbabwe, for the myth that denied African builders.\n- Nok, for \"making does not need a civilisation\".\n- Mesopotamian, for F1.9a's account.\n",
"- Polynesian, so the Pacific has one.\n\n## Movements, styles, schools, makers and institutions\n\nThe canon holds 333 movements, styles and schools and 218 maker communities, institutions and exhibitions, from every world. Mingei stops being the only movement with a page. The 21 drafted walks lean toward Europe and East Asia, so I recommend promoting six more so that F1.20's \"modernisms, plural\" is plural in its own walks.\n\n| World | Movements, styles, schools | Makers, institutions, events |\n|---|---|---|\n| Europe and the Mediterranean | 112 | 63 |\n| The Americas | 76 | 46 |\n| South and Southeast Asia | 54 | 28 |\n| Networks (cross-world) | 43 | 42 |\n| Africa | 42 | 34 |\n| East Asia | 42 | 24 |\n| The Islamic world | 37 | 18 |\n| Australia and the Pacific | 17 | 15 |\n| The steppe and Central Asia | 16 | 3 |\n| West Asia before Islam | 10 | 3 |\n\nA row counts in each world it belongs to. Europe still leads, because the field named most of its styles; the counts make that bias visible rather than hide it.\n\n",
"**Walks as drafted (29).** Movements and styles: Classical Greek, Gothic, Italian Renaissance, British design reform, Arts and Crafts, Art Nouveau, Deutscher Werkbund, Constructivism, Primitivism, Mexican Muralism, Benin court style, Mughal painting, the Pandit Seu family workshop (Guler), Swadeshi, the Indian craft revival, literati painting, the Kano school, ukiyo-e, chanoyu (wabi), Mingei, the Western Desert painting movement. Makers and institutions: Igun Eronmwon, the Jingdezhen imperial kilns, the Mughal karkhanas, Wedgwood's Etruria works, the Bauhaus, Ford, the Great Exhibition of 1851, and the Deir el-Medina workmen.\n\n**Six to promote for F1.20:** the Zaria Art Society (Natural Synthesis), the Casablanca School, Hurufiyya, Santiniketan, Antropofagia with Neo-Concretism, and Tropical Modernism. All six are in the canon at R2 or R3 today.\n\nThe canon also names what each movement leaves out: the anonymous makers it championed, its women members, the colonies it drew on. That field becomes the \"what it leaves out\" line on every grouping card.\n\n## Objects: from 64 records to about 700–900 verified\n\n",
"The world-set register holds 64 objects today, 45 of them exemplars. Five layers take Ring 1 to about 700–900 verified objects, each with a licence, a 1970 provenance test and a sensitivity check. The layers overlap, so the total is an estimate to be confirmed by the first harvest.\n\n| Layer | Rule | Estimate |\n|---|---|---|\n| Taught sets | 8–10 exemplars per world chapter, 10 chapters including F1.13 | 80–100 |\n| Thematic units | About five per unit across F1.1–F1.5 and F1.14–F1.31 | 120–140 |\n| World hubs | 20–40 reference objects per world (Deep Dives 1, option C) | 180–360 |\n| Cell floor | At least one verified object in every sub-region × period cell that had people in it (502 of 506) | 150–250 beyond the layers above |\n| Object-type floor | At least one verified example of each of the 323 object types | 80–120 beyond the layers above |\n\nRing 2 then takes every open record the harvest matches to a canon node, likely in the tens of thousands. Ring 3 is search across the connected open collections. At Ring 3, \"all objects\" means all the open ones, with the rest linked out.\n\nThree rules keep this honest:\n\n",
"- **The record's bias is shown, not hidden.** Where no licensed object exists (most twentieth-century community-made work, much of Oceania), the cell shows a living practice, a drawing of our own or a link out. The gap is labelled as the record's gap.\n- **Licence per record.** An anonymous maker or an old date proves nothing (finding 3.3). The Met's public-domain filter returns non-public-domain objects, so every hit is checked at record level.\n- **The recall deck stays at 60.** It is the Orientation spine; world hubs get their own optional decks of about 20.\n\n## Coverage: 485 of 506 cells hold three or more nodes\n\nThe grid grows from 43 sub-regions to 46, crossed with 11 period bands, for 506 cells. The four sub-regions Deep Dives 2 found absent now have canon rows: the Northwest Coast (25), the Eastern Woodlands (128), Madagascar and the Indian Ocean islands (29), and the Himalaya (47). This counts canon nodes, not verified objects; the object floor in §5 is the next test.\n\n| Macro-region | Sub-regions | Cells | 3+ nodes | 1–2 nodes | Empty |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Africa | 10 | 110 | 106 | 2 | 2 |\n| The Americas | 10 | 110 | 104 | 5 | 1 |\n| Asia | 9 | 99 | 95 | 3 | 1 |\n",
"| Western Asia | 6 | 66 | 64 | 2 | 0 |\n| Europe | 7 | 77 | 76 | 1 | 0 |\n| Oceania | 4 | 44 | 40 | 2 | 2 |\n\nThe six empty cells are mostly real absences, not gaps:\n\n- Micronesia before 4000 BCE: no people yet (settled about 1500 BCE).\n- Madagascar before 4000 BCE: human arrival is debated; the cell carries the debate, not an object.\n- Colonial and modern Latin America before 8000 BCE: the sub-region starts in 1492.\n- The Himalaya before 8000 BCE: a real gap. Denisovan presence on the Tibetan Plateau (Baishiya Karst Cave) is the candidate node.\n\nThe 15 thin cells are almost all deep time (before 8000 BCE), where the record itself is thin. The grid lives in the workbook's Coverage grid sheet, and recounts itself as rows are added.\n\nTwo code decisions are folded into Q30: Taiwan has no code of its own (coded AS-CHN and AS-ISE for now), and the Maldives sit under Madagascar and the Indian Ocean islands.\n\n## Zero cost: every budget line has a free replacement\n\n",
"F1's documents assumed money or paid access in eight places. Each now has a free route. Zero cost swaps money for time and for gates: where a paid step would have bought certainty, a free one buys it by waiting, or by not publishing until a reader clears the material.\n\n| Item | Was | Now, at zero cost | What it costs instead |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Steppe images (Pazyryk and others at the Hermitage) | Budget for permissions (Deep Dives 2, Q16) | Open objects of the same traditions (Met CC0 Ordos and Scythian metalwork, Cleveland, Walters); photographers' CC BY and CC BY-SA images on Commons; our own drawings of the designs; a link to the Hermitage page | Commons images need attribution and, for BY-SA, the same licence on anything adapted |\n| Pacific images | Budget for permissions from national museums | Te Papa and Auckland items marked reusable, except taonga, which their own rules keep to research use; Smithsonian CC0 where marked; Tropenmuseum's CC BY-SA files on Commons; community-published material; living practice instead of object | Fewer objects; more practices and links; the gap is shown |\n",
"| Sensitivity reads for F1.6, F1.12 and F1.13 | About $2,000–2,500 when budget exists (Phase 4 item 15, option A) | The Historian pre-read (D) for all chapters; reciprocal reviewers (B) credited by name, with founding access and the chapter for their own teaching; university course partnerships where students review a world chapter; open \"under review\" publishing (C) only after a B reader clears the chapter | Slower. The three riskiest chapters hold back sacred and contested steps until a reader clears them. Community members are never asked for unpaid labour; their published sources are used and credited |\n| Traditional Knowledge Notices | Local Contexts assumed free | Local Contexts' free Individual researcher account: one user, ten projects a year — one per world chapter plus F1.13. Labels are applied by communities themselves, at no cost to us | Notices placed by hand (no API); paid tiers ($1,250–$10,000 a year) not needed |\n| Scholarship behind paywalls | Assumed library access | Open access first (Unpaywall, CORE, DOAJ, OpenAlex); JSTOR's free account for the rest | JSTOR's free reading is now 10 articles every 30 days; plan reads per chapter |\n",
"| Untranslated primary texts (Yingzao Fashi, Muqaddimah) | Paraphrase only | Our own working translations of passages from the public-domain originals, labelled as such and checked against published scholarship | Must be marked \"F1 working translation\"; short passages only |\n| Harvest runtime and hosting | Free tiers (Phase 4 item 11) | Unchanged: scheduled jobs in a public repository writing JSON and CSV; static hosting; free database tier for learners | Limits checked at build time, each with a named fallback |\n| API keys | Smithsonian key | Free through api.data.gov | None |\n\n",
"Sources: [Local Contexts subscriptions](https://localcontexts.org/hub-agreements/subscribers/), [JSTOR free access](https://support.jstor.org/hc/en-us/articles/115004760028-How-to-Register-Get-Free-Access-to-Content), [Smithsonian Open Access FAQ](https://www.si.edu/openaccess/faq), [Te Papa](https://www.tepapa.govt.nz/about-collections-online), [Auckland Museum](https://www.aucklandmuseum.com/discover/collections/using-our-images), [Tropenmuseum on Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:KIT-license). The Hermitage's own image terms could not be read (its page would not load), so it stays link-only.\n\n## The free source ladder\n\nThe registry lists 198 free routes. 103 were checked live on 28 September 2026: 74 fully and 29 in part. 60 are safe for commercial use, 103 are safe item by item, and 35 are non-commercial only. Each medium is taken from the highest rung that has it.\n\n1. **Open museum records (CC0 or public domain):** Cleveland, the Met, the Art Institute of Chicago, Smithsonian items marked CC0, the Rijksmuseum, the National Gallery of Art, the Walters, SMK and Paris Musées.\n",
"2. **National open licences:** KOGL Type 1 (Korea, item by item), ColBase under Japan's Government Standard Terms, and the National Palace Museum in Taipei.\n3. **Attribution licences (CC BY, CC BY-SA):** photographers on Wikimedia Commons, Tropenmuseum's 51,506 files, Auckland Museum's non-taonga images, Wellcome, and figures from open-access papers.\n4. **Our own production:** drawings, maps, timelines, diagrams and working translations (§9).\n5. **Link out, never host:** anything non-commercial, no-derivatives, permission-only, or restricted by a community's rules.\n\nThe data backbone is free and reusable: Wikidata and PeriodO (CC0), Pleiades and geoBoundaries (CC BY), the Getty vocabularies (ODC-By, credit required), Cliopatria, Natural Earth (public domain) and UNESCO's intangible heritage data.\n\nFive findings from the licence checks change plans:\n\n- **Many big \"free\" routes are non-commercial.** These are the British Museum (images and its Sketchfab models), the V&A, Gallica, Digital Bodleian, Smarthistory, EMKP, Seshat, the International Dunhuang Programme, the Qatar Digital Library, SlaveVoyages' estimates, and David Rumsey maps. Q28 decides whether F1 can ever host them.\n",
"- **Community rules override open licences.** Te Papa keeps taonga Māori images to research and education, and Auckland Museum keeps Māori and Pacific images to personal research. Digital Benin keeps each lending museum's licence. Korea's e-museum is non-commercial unless an item is marked KOGL Type 1.\n- **3D is item by item.** Smithsonian models are CC0 only where marked. Sketchfab's CC0 programme and Scan the World vary by model. Only models marked CC0 are hosted.\n- **Access has tightened.** JSTOR's free reading dropped to 10 articles a month. OpenAlex needs a key and gives $1 of free use a day. Trove bars downloading content. DOAJ now charges for up-to-date bulk feeds, while its API stays free.\n- **Facts typed from memory go stale.** 19 of 124 UNESCO statuses in the first draft were wrong: elements moved between lists, were extended or were renamed. UNESCO status and licences now come from the harvest, never from typing (Q34).\n\nThe full registry, with the page each licence was read from, is the workbook's Sources sheet.\n\n## Making visuals at zero cost\n\nWhat cannot be licensed can often be drawn. Every asset type has a free route, and a rule that keeps it honest.\n\n",
"| Asset | Free route | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Object images | Ladder rungs 1–3 (§8), shown through IIIF deep zoom where the source offers it | Licence and credit line checked per record, each January when collections update |\n| Maps and borders | Natural Earth base; geoBoundaries for modern borders; Cliopatria for historical polities; Pleiades for ancient places; drawn with MapLibre and our own tiles | Historical name first, modern country second; the borders layer states that it maps states only |\n| Timelines, grids, networks | Our own SVG, drawn from the registers with D3 or Observable Plot | Date ranges never drawn as points; human flows never drawn as goods |\n| Pattern and technique drawings | Our own line drawings of public-domain designs and our own operational-sequence strips | Drawn from several sources and labelled \"drawing after\"; never traced from one copyrighted photograph |\n| 3D | Models marked CC0 (Smithsonian, Sketchfab's CC0 programme); others linked | NC models (the British Museum's) are linked, never embedded in a paid product |\n",
"| Sound | The National Jukebox's pre-1923 recordings; Freesound CC0; our own narration | 1923 line checked per recording; other countries noted per item |\n| Film | Public-domain films on the Internet Archive (the Prelinger collection), Library of Congress films, NASA imagery; our own motion graphics from open images | British Pathé and Folkways are paid for reuse, so they are linked only |\n| Type and script | Noto fonts (Open Font License) for every script F1 shows, which keeps the app ready for Arabic | None |\n| Reconstructions | Our own diagrams, built from cited evidence and labelled as reconstructions | See Q32 on generated images |\n\nOne thing zero cost does not buy is a fresh photograph of an object we cannot license. There the Atlas shows the drawing, the practice or the link, and says why.\n\n## All recommendations, modified and expanded\n\nThis list replaces my earlier recommendations. It keeps what held from tasks 25–27, including the licence routes, the export-law line and the colonial-context screen, and adds what the full canon makes possible.\n\n**The Atlas and its pages**\n\n",
"1. Keep the ten world and network chapters, each with its device. Put breadth in world hubs: one hub page for each of the 46 sub-regions, built as a grouping page of kind \"region\".\n2. Give all 93 civilisation names a card and a grouping page, with 13 walks (§3).\n3. Raise grouping-page walks from the planned 60 to about 210: the 205 drafted, the six F1.20 promotions, and the civilisation changes.\n4. Add twelve function strands, one walk per function across all worlds. \"Care and access\" carries the body-and-access lens.\n5. Add technique strands: the 13 R1 techniques as operational-sequence walks, which F1.25's fifth layer and F1.28a reuse.\n6. Load the Time surface's \"first known\" mode with the 174 claims. Independent inventions sit on parallel rows, as F1.5's simultaneity view needs.\n7. End every world chapter on a living practice, chosen from the 214. UNESCO status is harvested, never typed.\n8. Apply the human-flow rule to all 161 rows flagged for it, not only the Atlantic.\n\n**Objects and data**\n\n1. Build Ring 1 to about 700–900 verified objects in the five layers of §5, with a floor per cell and per object type.\n",
"2. Take every medium from the highest rung of the source ladder (§8); anything below rung 4 is linked, never hosted.\n3. Add four fields to every world-set record: licence route (the six rungs), sensitivity class (the eight values in the canon), Notice or Label, and canon IDs (the groupings it belongs to).\n4. Keep the colonial-context screen for objects made after 1800 in colonised regions. Keep the export-law line on every card (Iraq 1936, Egypt 1983, UNESCO 1970).\n5. Let the harvest jobs match every canon row to Wikidata, Getty and PeriodO identifiers. No identifier is ever typed from memory. Licences are rechecked every January.\n6. Treat the canon CSVs as the source of truth. The drafting scripts are archived, because rerunning them would erase the fact-check fixes.\n\n**Specs, voice and review**\n\n1. Deep-dive template v3 (Groundwork Q19 and Q23) also adds a responsibility block (which cells, object types and canon groupings the unit must reach), the \"what it leaves out\" line, and the four record fields above.\n",
"2. Historian pack v2 (Groundwork Q24) also briefs the Historian voice as a regional specialist for each world. It checks five things: a \"leaves out\" line on every grouping, no ranking of civilisations, the human-flow rule, the Q21 funerary rule, and the confidence grammar. It also flags any typed fact that should come from a harvest.\n3. Before G3, fact-check every R1 row, every UNESCO claim and every licence. In the first pass, 1 error turned up in 120 randomly sampled rows, but 19 in 124 UNESCO claims.\n4. Run sensitivity review the zero-cost way (§7), and hold back sacred and contested steps until a reader clears them.\n5. Keep the regional slot (F1.31) locally authored. The Gulf and MENA expansion later draws on the same canon: Magan, Dilmun, Al Sadu, Hafit and Umm an-Nar rows are already in it.\n6. Record Q27–Q35 in the Program Hub, the only place where decisions change.\n\n## Delivery order\n\nNothing in the scope is cut. The order only decides what a tester sees first, and every phase adds to the one before. On day one, every one of the 2,430 nodes exists as at least an R3 card, because R3 cards are generated from register rows.\n\n",
"1. **Now, needing no decision:** set up the harvest runtime. Match the canon rows to Wikidata, Getty and PeriodO. Load the UNESCO intangible heritage dataset. Harvest Ring 1 candidates for the taught sets. Run the first object coverage report against the 506 cells.\n2. **Before G3:** fact-check every R1 row. Write unit specs in batches on template v3. Write the 13 civilisation walks and the walks the units need first. Send F1.6, F1.12 and F1.13 to reciprocal reviewers.\n3. **Atlas v1 (static):** Place, Time, Connections, Story and Grouping pages. All nodes at R3 or above, civilisation names at R2, and the taught sets and thematic-unit objects verified.\n4. **Atlas v1.x, world by world:** world hubs, then the cell floor and the object-type floor, the remaining walks, and the function and technique strands.\n5. **Later versions:** the Ring 2 harvest at scale, Compare, the calendar and lineage modes, and the Gulf and MENA vertical.\n\n## Decisions for you\n\nNine questions, numbered on from Groundwork's Q26. Groundwork Q19–Q26 (provenance fields, the funerary rule, template v3, Historian pack v2) still stand as written. \"Agree to all\" or a list of exceptions is enough.\n\n",
"| # | Question | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Q27 | Make zero cost a hard rule for research, sources and visuals, dropping Deep Dives 2 Q16's permissions budget and item 15's option A? | Yes |\n| Q28 | Host only media that is safe for commercial use, and link the rest? | Yes. If you can commit that F1 will never charge, and is not run for profit, the 35 non-commercial routes can be hosted. That would add the British Museum, the V&A, EMKP and Smarthistory, and fill much of Africa and Oceania. Creative Commons treats anything \"primarily intended for commercial advantage\" as commercial, so a company-run course would likely count |\n| Q29 | Define \"everything\" as every canon node on the Atlas at R3 or above, all 93 civilisation names at R2 with 13 walks, and about 210 walks in all? | Yes |\n| Q30 | Adopt the 46 sub-regions and 11 period bands in the workbook? | Yes. Code Taiwan by the world it belonged to at the time: AS-ISE for Austronesian material, AS-CHN from the Qing period. Keep the Maldives with the Indian Ocean islands |\n| Q31 | Set Ring 1 at about 700–900 verified objects in five layers, with a floor per cell and per object type? | Yes, confirmed after the first harvest |\n",
"| Q32 | Generated images: none of historical objects, people or places, and only diagrams drawn from cited sources? | Yes. A generated \"reconstruction\" is indistinguishable from evidence to a learner |\n| Q33 | Allow our own working translations of public-domain originals, labelled and checked against scholarship? | Yes, short passages only |\n| Q34 | Take UNESCO status, licences and identifiers only from harvests, never from typing? | Yes |\n| Q35 | Make the canon CSVs the source of truth, versioned with the registers, with a review log for every change? | Yes |\n\n## Files and sources\n\n- **F1_canon_register_v1.xlsx:** Summary, Canon (2,430 rows), Coverage grid, Functions x worlds, Sources (198) and Primary texts (130). The counts are formulas, so they update as rows change.\n- **F1_canon_v1.zip:** the same data as CSV files for the data repository, plus the two fact-check logs and the licence verification notes.\n\nHow it was built: six drafting passes wrote the families in parallel. Two independent checkers then read about 620 rows against scholarly, museum and UNESCO pages and corrected 66 of them. No identifiers were guessed.\n\n",
"Sources for the licence and access facts in §7 and §8, read on 28 September 2026:\n\n- [Cleveland Museum of Art Open Access](https://www.clevelandart.org/open-access)\n- [Smithsonian Open Access FAQ](https://www.si.edu/openaccess/faq)\n- [Smithsonian 3D open access](https://3d.si.edu/collections/openaccesshighlights)\n- [British Museum copyright and permissions](https://www.britishmuseum.org/terms-use/copyright-and-permissions)\n- [V&A terms](https://www.vam.ac.uk/info/va-websites-terms-conditions/)\n- [Gallica conditions of use](https://gallica.bnf.fr/accueil/fr/html/conditions-dutilisation-de-gallica)\n- [EMKP repository](https://www.emkp.org/repository/)\n- [Smarthistory FAQ](https://smarthistory.org/faq/)\n- [Te Papa collections online](https://www.tepapa.govt.nz/about-collections-online)\n- [Auckland Museum image use](https://www.aucklandmuseum.com/discover/collections/using-our-images)\n- [Tropenmuseum files on Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:KIT-license)\n- [Local Contexts subscriptions](https://localcontexts.org/hub-agreements/subscribers/)\n- [JSTOR free access](https://support.jstor.org/hc/en-us/articles/115004760028-How-to-Register-Get-Free-Access-to-Content)\n",
"- [OpenAlex pricing](https://help.openalex.org/access/pricing/)\n- [National Jukebox rights](https://www.loc.gov/collections/national-jukebox/about-this-collection/rights-and-access/)\n- [PeriodO licence](https://perio.do/license/)\n- [Getty vocabularies](https://www.getty.edu/research/tools/vocabularies/lod/index.html)\n- [Cliopatria licence](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria/main/LICENSE.md)\n- [Natural Earth terms](https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/)\n- [geoBoundaries licence](https://github.com/wmgeolab/geoBoundaries/blob/main/LICENSE)\n- [Noto fonts licence](https://github.com/notofonts/noto-fonts/blob/main/LICENSE)\n- [UNESCO intangible heritage open data](https://ich.unesco.org/fr/acces-libre-aux-donnees-de-dive-01218)\n"
